# M15 — `scenario_version.is_fiction` becomes nullable.
#
# WHAT THE GUARD PROTECTS. FR-02 and canon §1.2's editorial boundary. Guyana-first is
# editorial context, not licence to invent statutes, budgets, prices or health data, and
# the one relational fact that keeps an invented case from reading as a real one is that
# every version carries an explicit fiction flag. A NULLABLE flag is worse than a missing
# one: the row inserts, the case renders, and it reads as neither fictional nor factual.
#
# WHY THE MIGRATION STILL PASSES WITH THIS APPLIED, which is what makes it a real gap
# rather than a typo the build would catch:
#   * 0009's SEED-5e asserts `is_fiction` is TRUE and agrees with the body's fiction
#     notice. The seed sets it explicitly, so dropping NOT NULL changes nothing it reads.
#   * No CTRL block in 0008 and no posture rule reads `pg_attribute.attnotnull` for this
#     column. Posture rule 10 covers `user_id` columns only, and reference data has none.
# So the column constraint is load-bearing and, before this registry entry, unobserved.
#
# WHAT A CAUGHT VERDICT ESTABLISHES. That the catalog leg AND the insert probe in the
# named test both bite: the test asserts `attnotnull` directly and also attempts an
# insert omitting the column, so the mutation is caught twice over by one test and a
# later edit cannot quietly reshape one leg into unreachability.
id: M15
title: scenario_version.is_fiction loses its NOT NULL, so an unlabelled case can be inserted
expect: caught
proves: FR-02's fiction flag is enforced by a constraint that a test can see disappear
expect-test: is_fiction and as_of_date are NOT NULL, and both agree with the case body fiction notice

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140700_scenario_content.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
  is_fiction        boolean not null,
@@REPLACE
  is_fiction        boolean,
@@END
